select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 3, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.0", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.0 Overview", "body": "CME Group. Contract specifications for copper, gold, crude oil, E-mini S&P 500, and Euro FX futures. Chicago: CME Group.\nCulp, C. L., and Miller, M. H. (1995). Metallgesellschaft and the economics of synthetic storage. Journal of Applied Corporate Finance, 7(4), 62–76.\nDuffie, D., and Zhu, H. (2011). Does a central clearing counterparty reduce counterparty risk? Review of Asset Pricing Studies, 1(1), 74–95.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 2.\nPirrong, C. (2011). The Economics of Central Clearing: Theory and Practice. ISDA Discussion Paper No. 1."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.0", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nWhich bank offers Northwind the highest return?\nNorthwind's treasurer has $10,000,000 to deposit for one year and receives three quotations. The three banks are of equal credit quality. Select the deposit that produces the most interest."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.1", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.1", "heading": "Interest rates in derivatives markets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.1 Interest rates in derivatives markets", "body": "An interest rate is the amount a borrower promises to pay a lender for the use of money, expressed as a fraction of the amount lent per unit of time. Many interest rates are quoted in any currency at any time, and they differ chiefly in credit risk, the risk that the borrower will not pay as promised. The greater the credit risk, the higher the rate. Four kinds of rate are important in the valuation of derivatives.\nTreasury rates are the rates at which a government borrows in its own currency by issuing bills and bonds. Because a government can ordinarily meet obligations in its own currency, these rates are regarded as free of credit risk."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.1", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.1", "heading": "Interest rates in derivatives markets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.1 Interest rates in derivatives markets", "body": "Overnight reference rates are published daily and measure the cost of borrowing for one business day. Each major currency has one: the Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR) for the United States dollar, the Sterling Overnight Index Average (SONIA) for the pound, the Euro Short-Term Rate (€STR) for the euro, and the Tokyo Overnight Average Rate (TONA) for the yen. These rates have replaced the London Interbank Offered Rate (LIBOR), which was based on estimates submitted by banks and not on transactions; publication of the principal dollar LIBOR rates on that basis ended in June 2023. Floating payments in swaps are now determined by compounding the overnight rate over each payment period.\nRepo rates are rates on repurchase agreements, in which one party sells securities and agrees to repurchase them later at a slightly higher price. The transaction is in substance a loan secured by the securities, and the rate is correspondingly low. SOFR is computed from overnight repo transactions secured by Treasury securities."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.1", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.1", "heading": "Interest rates in derivatives markets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.1 Interest rates in derivatives markets", "body": "The risk-free rate. The valuation of derivatives requires a risk-free rate, the rate earned on an investment with no credit risk. Market practice is to use rates derived from overnight indexed swaps (OIS), contracts in which a fixed rate is exchanged for the compounded overnight reference rate; Chapter 7 describes them. For the present it is sufficient to know that a term structure of risk-free rates can be observed in the market for every major currency. Throughout this book, \\(r\\) denotes the risk-free rate."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.2", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.2", "heading": "Measuring interest rates: compounding", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.2 Measuring interest rates: compounding", "body": "A statement that an interest rate is 5 percent per year is incomplete. Its meaning depends on how often interest is added to the principal, which is the compounding frequency. If the rate \\(R\\) is compounded \\(m\\) times per year, an amount \\(A\\) invested for \\(n\\) years grows to\n\\[ A\\left(1+\\frac{R}{m}\\right)^{mn}. \\]\nWith \\(m=1\\) the rate is compounded annually; with \\(m=2\\), semiannually; with \\(m=4\\), quarterly. The effective annual rate is the rate with annual compounding that produces the same result: \\((1+R/m)^m-1\\). The opening exercise turned on this point. A rate of 4.92 percent compounded quarterly has an effective annual rate of \\((1.0123)^4-1=5.0115\\) percent, which exceeds 5.00 percent compounded annually."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.2", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.2", "heading": "Continuous compounding", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.2 Continuous compounding", "body": "As the compounding frequency \\(m\\) increases without limit, the terminal value approaches a limit. Because \\(\\lim_{m\\to\\infty}(1+R/m)^{m}=e^{R}\\), where \\(e=2.71828\\ldots\\) is the base of natural logarithms, an amount \\(A\\) invested for \\(n\\) years at a rate \\(R\\) with continuous compounding grows to\n\\[ A\\,e^{Rn}. \\]\nConversely, the value today of an amount \\(A\\) to be received in \\(n\\) years is \\(A\\,e^{-Rn}\\). The operation of converting a future amount into its value today is discounting, and the factor \\(e^{-Rn}\\) is the discount factor. Continuous compounding is used throughout the theory of derivatives because it makes these two operations simple: compounding over successive periods adds the exponents, and discounting is multiplication by an exponential. For practical purposes it is nearly indistinguishable from daily compounding."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.2", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.2", "heading": "Conversion", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.2 Conversion", "body": "Let \\(R_c\\) be a rate with continuous compounding and \\(R_m\\) the equivalent rate compounded \\(m\\) times per year. The two produce the same terminal value when \\(e^{R_c}=(1+R_m/m)^m\\), which gives\n\\[ R_c = m\\,\\ln\\!\\left(1+\\frac{R_m}{m}\\right), \\qquad R_m = m\\left(e^{R_c/m}-1\\right). \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.2", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.2", "heading": "Conversion", "kind": "example", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.2 Conversion", "body": "Worked example 3.1 — Converting between conventions\n(a) A bond yield is quoted as 6 percent with semiannual compounding. Find the equivalent continuously compounded rate. (b) A lender quotes 5 percent with continuous compounding on a loan with interest paid quarterly. Find the rate with quarterly compounding and the interest on $1,000,000 each quarter.\n1\n(a) \\(R_c=2\\ln(1+0.06/2)=2\\ln(1.03)=2\\times0.029559=0.059118\\), or 5.912 percent.\n2\n(b) \\(R_4=4\\left(e^{0.05/4}-1\\right)=4\\times0.012578=0.050314\\), or 5.031 percent.\n3\nInterest each quarter: \\(1{,}000{,}000\\times0.050314/4=\\$12{,}578\\).\n4\nObservation. For a given investment, the more frequent the compounding, the lower the quoted rate that describes it. A continuously compounded rate is always below the equivalent rate under any discrete convention."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.2#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.2", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.2", "heading": "Conversion", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.2 Conversion", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 3.1 — Compounding frequency\nOne quoted rate, six meanings\nSet a quoted rate and a horizon. The table shows the terminal value of $1,000 under each compounding convention, the effective annual rate, and the continuously compounded rate that is equivalent to the quotation under that convention."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.2#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.2", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.2", "heading": "Conversion", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.2 Conversion", "body": "Concept check 3.1\nAn investment of $5,000 is worth $5,600 after two years. What is the continuously compounded rate of return per year?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.3", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.3", "heading": "Zero rates and the pricing of bonds", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.3 Zero rates and the pricing of bonds", "body": "The \\(n\\)-year zero rate, also called the \\(n\\)-year spot rate, is the rate of interest earned on an investment that begins today and lasts \\(n\\) years, with all interest and principal paid at the end and nothing paid in between. It is the yield on a zero-coupon bond, a bond that makes a single payment at maturity. The relation between the zero rate and maturity is the zero curve, or the term structure of interest rates.\nMost bonds pay coupons, periodic payments of interest, and repay the principal (also called the face value or par value) at maturity. A coupon bond is a collection of single payments at different dates, and each payment should be discounted at the zero rate for its own date. The price of the bond is the sum of the present values.\nTable 3.1 Zero rates used in the examples of this section (continuous compounding)\nMaturity (years)Zero rateDiscount factor\n0.54.0%0.980199\n1.04.4%0.956954\n1.54.7%0.931928\n2.04.9%0.906649"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.3", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.3", "heading": "Zero rates and the pricing of bonds", "kind": "example", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.3 Zero rates and the pricing of bonds", "body": "Worked example 3.2 — Pricing a coupon bond from zero rates\nA two-year bond with a principal of $100 pays a coupon of 5 percent per year, semiannually. Find its price using the zero rates of Table 3.1.\n1\nCash flows. $2.50 at 0.5, 1.0, and 1.5 years; $102.50 at 2.0 years.\n2\nPresent values. \\(2.50\\times0.980199=2.4505\\); \\(2.50\\times0.956954=2.3924\\); \\(2.50\\times0.931928=2.3298\\); \\(102.50\\times0.906649=92.9315\\).\n3\nPrice. \\(2.4505+2.3924+2.3298+92.9315=\\$100.10\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.3", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.3", "heading": "Bond yield and par yield", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.3 Bond yield and par yield", "body": "The yield of a bond, or yield to maturity, is the single discount rate that, applied to every cash flow, gives the market price. For the bond of Worked example 3.2 the yield \\(y\\), with continuous compounding, solves\n\\[ 2.50\\,e^{-0.5y}+2.50\\,e^{-1.0y}+2.50\\,e^{-1.5y}+102.50\\,e^{-2.0y}=100.10 . \\]\nThe equation has no algebraic solution and is solved by trial and error or by a numerical routine; the result is \\(y=4.884\\) percent. The yield is an average of the zero rates, weighted toward the two-year rate because most of the value of the bond lies in the final payment.\nThe par yield for a given maturity is the coupon rate that causes a bond to be priced at its principal, or at par. Let \\(d_i\\) denote the discount factor for the \\(i\\)th payment date, \\(d_n\\) the discount factor for maturity, \\(A=\\sum d_i\\) the sum of the discount factors on all payment dates, and \\(m\\) the number of payments per year. A bond with principal 100 and annual coupon \\(c\\) is priced at par when \\(100=(c/m)A+100\\,d_n\\), so that\n\\[ c=\\frac{(100-100\\,d_n)\\,m}{A}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.3#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.3", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.3", "heading": "Bond yield and par yield", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.3 Bond yield and par yield", "body": "With the rates of Table 3.1, \\(A=0.980199+0.956954+0.931928+0.906649=3.775729\\) and \\(c=(100-90.6649)\\times2/3.775729=4.945\\). The two-year par yield is 4.945 percent with semiannual compounding. The coupon of 5 percent in Worked example 3.2 is slightly above this, which is why that bond is priced slightly above par."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.4", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.4", "heading": "Determining zero rates: the bootstrap method", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.4 Determining zero rates: the bootstrap method", "body": "Zero rates for short maturities can be observed directly from the prices of Treasury bills, which pay no coupon. For longer maturities few zero-coupon instruments trade, and the zero rates must be inferred from the prices of coupon bonds. The standard procedure, the bootstrap method, proceeds from the shortest maturity to the longest, using the zero rates already found to isolate one new rate at each step.\nTable 3.2 Data for the bootstrap method (principal $100; coupons paid semiannually)\nBondMaturity (years)Annual couponPriceZero rate obtained\nA0.250$99.004.020%\nB0.500$97.904.245%\nC1.000$95.604.500%\nD1.50$5.00$100.404.672%\nE2.00$5.50$101.004.923%"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.4", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.4", "heading": "Determining zero rates: the bootstrap method", "kind": "example", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.4 Determining zero rates: the bootstrap method", "body": "Worked example 3.3 — Bootstrapping the zero curve\nDerive the zero rates in the last column of Table 3.2.\n1\nBonds A, B, and C make a single payment of $100, so each price is a discount factor multiplied by 100. For bond A, \\(100e^{-0.25R}=99.00\\) gives \\(R=-\\ln(0.9900)/0.25=4.020\\%\\). Similarly \\(R=-\\ln(0.9790)/0.5=4.245\\%\\) for B and \\(R=-\\ln(0.9560)/1.0=4.500\\%\\) for C.\n2\nBond D pays $2.50 at 0.5 and 1.0 years and $102.50 at 1.5 years. The first two payments are discounted with factors already known, 0.9790 and 0.9560: \\(2.50\\times0.9790+2.50\\times0.9560=4.8375\\).\n3\nThe final payment must account for the rest of the price: \\(102.50\\,d_{1.5}=100.40-4.8375=95.5625\\), so \\(d_{1.5}=0.932317\\) and \\(R=-\\ln(0.932317)/1.5=4.672\\%\\).\n4\nBond E pays $2.75 at 0.5, 1.0, and 1.5 years and $102.75 at 2.0 years. \\(2.75\\times(0.9790+0.9560+0.932317)=7.8851\\). Then \\(102.75\\,d_{2}=101.00-7.8851=93.1149\\), so \\(d_2=0.906228\\) and \\(R=-\\ln(0.906228)/2=4.923\\%\\).\n5\nCompletion. Rates for maturities between those computed are obtained by interpolation, most simply by joining the computed points with straight lines."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.5#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.5", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.5", "heading": "Forward rates", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.5 Forward rates", "body": "A forward rate is the rate of interest, implied by today's zero rates, for a period of time that begins in the future. Suppose that the zero rate for maturity \\(T_1\\) is \\(R_1\\) and the zero rate for a longer maturity \\(T_2\\) is \\(R_2\\), both with continuous compounding. An investment for \\(T_2\\) years at \\(R_2\\) must produce the same result as an investment for \\(T_1\\) years at \\(R_1\\) followed by an investment from \\(T_1\\) to \\(T_2\\) at the forward rate \\(R_F\\); otherwise one of the two would be preferred by every investor. Hence \\(e^{R_2T_2}=e^{R_1T_1}\\,e^{R_F(T_2-T_1)}\\), and\n\\[ R_F=\\frac{R_2T_2-R_1T_1}{T_2-T_1}. \\]\nTable 3.3 Zero rates and the forward rates they imply (continuous compounding)\nYear \\(n\\)Zero rate for an \\(n\\)-year investmentForward rate for year \\(n\\)\n13.2%—\n23.8%4.4%\n34.2%5.0%\n44.5%5.4%\n54.7%5.5%\nThe forward rate for the second year is \\((3.8\\times2-3.2\\times1)/(2-1)=4.4\\) percent, and that for the fifth year is \\((4.7\\times5-4.5\\times4)/1=5.5\\) percent. The formula may be rewritten as\n\\[ R_F=R_2+(R_2-R_1)\\frac{T_1}{T_2-T_1}, \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.5#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.5", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.5", "heading": "Forward rates", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.5 Forward rates", "body": "which shows that when the zero curve slopes upward, so that \\(R_2>R_1\\), the forward rate exceeds both zero rates; when the zero curve slopes downward, the forward rate lies below them. The reasoning is that of averages: a zero rate is the average of the forward rates over its life, and for an average to rise the newest element must exceed it.\nA forward rate is not merely a calculation. An investor can secure it today. Using Table 3.3, a company that borrows $100 for one year at 3.2 percent and invests the proceeds for two years at 3.8 percent has no net cash flow today, owes \\(100e^{0.032}=\\$103.25\\) at the end of year 1, and receives \\(100e^{0.076}=\\$107.90\\) at the end of year 2. It has arranged, today, to invest $103.25 during the second year at the rate \\(\\ln(107.90/103.25)=4.4\\) percent. The contract that provides this result directly, the forward rate agreement, is treated in Chapter 6."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.5#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.5", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.5", "heading": "Forward rates", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.5 Forward rates", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 3.2 — Zero curve and forward curve\nThe forward curve lies above a rising zero curve and below a falling one\nSet the zero rate at the short end and the level that long-maturity rates approach. The solid line is the zero curve; the dashed line is the forward rate for a very short period beginning at each date. Make the curve flat, then inverted, and observe the position of the forward curve."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.5#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.5", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.5", "heading": "Forward rates", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.5 Forward rates", "body": "★ Optional: the instantaneous forward rate. Let \\(R(T)\\) be the zero rate for maturity \\(T\\). Setting \\(T_1=T\\) and \\(T_2=T+\\Delta T\\) in the forward rate formula and letting \\(\\Delta T\\) approach zero gives the forward rate for a very short period beginning at \\(T\\): \\[ R_F(T)=\\frac{d}{dT}\\bigl[T\\,R(T)\\bigr]=R(T)+T\\,\\frac{dR}{dT}. \\] The forward curve in Interactive figure 3.2 is computed from this expression. It exceeds the zero rate exactly where the zero curve has positive slope. Equivalently, if \\(P(0,T)=e^{-R(T)T}\\) is the price today of a zero-coupon bond paying 1 at \\(T\\), then \\(R_F(T)=-\\partial\\ln P(0,T)/\\partial T\\), the form in which the forward rate appears in the interest rate models of advanced texts.\nConcept check 3.2\nThe three-year zero rate is 5.0 percent and the four-year zero rate is 4.8 percent, both with continuous compounding. What is the forward rate for the fourth year?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.6#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.6", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.6", "heading": "Discount factors in three dimensions", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.6 Discount factors in three dimensions", "body": "The discount factor \\(e^{-RT}\\) depends on two quantities, the rate and the maturity, and its behavior in the two together governs the valuation of every contract in later chapters. Interactive figure 3.3 presents it as a surface. Two features should be noted. For a short maturity the surface is almost level across rates: the value of a payment due soon is insensitive to the rate. For a long maturity the surface falls steeply as the rate rises: the value of a distant payment is highly sensitive to the rate. This observation is made precise by the concept of duration in the next section."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.6#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.6", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.6", "heading": "Discount factors in three dimensions", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.6 Discount factors in three dimensions", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 3.3 — Three-dimensional view\nThe discount factor as a function of maturity and rate\nDrag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom; the arrow keys also rotate the figure. The gold curve shows the discount factor across maturities at the selected rate.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.7#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.7", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.7", "heading": "Duration", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.7 Duration", "body": "The duration of a bond is the average time at which its cash flows are received, where each time is weighted by the share of the bond's value that the corresponding cash flow provides. Let the bond pay \\(c_i\\) at time \\(t_i\\), let \\(y\\) be its yield with continuous compounding, and let \\(B=\\sum c_ie^{-yt_i}\\) be its price. The duration is\n\\[ D=\\frac{\\sum_i t_i\\,c_i\\,e^{-yt_i}}{B}. \\]\nThe duration of a zero-coupon bond equals its maturity. The duration of a coupon bond is less than its maturity, because some of its value is received earlier. The importance of duration lies in the following result, obtained by differentiating the price with respect to the yield: \\(dB/dy=-\\sum t_ic_ie^{-yt_i}=-BD\\). For a small change \\(\\Delta y\\) in the yield,\n\\[ \\Delta B\\approx-B\\,D\\,\\Delta y, \\qquad\\text{that is,}\\qquad \\frac{\\Delta B}{B}\\approx-D\\,\\Delta y. \\]\nThe percentage change in the price of a bond is approximately its duration multiplied by the change in its yield, with the opposite sign. Duration is therefore a measure of exposure to interest rates, and it is the basis of the hedging methods of Chapter 6."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.7#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.7", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.7", "heading": "Duration", "kind": "example", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.7 Duration", "body": "Worked example 3.4 — Duration of the two-year bond\nCompute the duration of the bond of Worked example 3.2, whose price is $100.10 and yield 4.884 percent, and estimate the change in its price if the yield rises by 0.10 percentage points.\n1\nPresent values at the yield. \\(2.50e^{-0.04884\\times0.5}=2.4397\\); \\(2.50e^{-0.04884\\times1.0}=2.3808\\); \\(2.50e^{-0.04884\\times1.5}=2.3234\\); \\(102.50e^{-0.04884\\times2.0}=92.9603\\). Sum: 100.10.\n2\nTime-weighted sum. \\(0.5\\times2.4397+1.0\\times2.3808+1.5\\times2.3234+2.0\\times92.9603=193.006\\).\n3\nDuration. \\(D=193.006/100.10=1.928\\) years.\n4\nPrice change. \\(\\Delta B\\approx-100.10\\times1.928\\times0.001=-\\$0.193\\). Repricing the bond at a yield of 4.984 percent gives a change of −$0.1928; the approximation is accurate to the third decimal place."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.7#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.7", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.7", "heading": "Duration", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.7 Duration", "body": "When yields are expressed with compounding \\(m\\) times per year, the relation becomes \\(\\Delta B\\approx-BD\\,\\Delta y/(1+y/m)\\), and the quantity \\(D^{*}=D/(1+y/m)\\) is the modified duration. For large changes in yield the linear approximation is inadequate, because the relation between price and yield is curved. The curvature is measured by convexity, \\(C=\\sum t_i^{2}c_ie^{-yt_i}/B\\), and the improved approximation is \\(\\Delta B/B\\approx-D\\,\\Delta y+\\tfrac12C(\\Delta y)^2\\). For the bond above, \\(C=3.797\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.8#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.8", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.8", "heading": "Theories of the term structure", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.8 Theories of the term structure", "body": "Three theories seek to explain the shape of the zero curve. The expectations theory holds that a forward rate equals the short-term rate that the market expects to prevail in the corresponding future period; an upward-sloping curve then signals an expectation of rising rates. The market segmentation theory holds that short-, medium-, and long-term rates are determined in separate markets by the supply of and demand for funds of each maturity, with no necessary relation among them. The liquidity preference theory holds that investors prefer to lend for short periods and borrowers prefer to borrow for long periods, so that long-term rates must include a premium to induce investors to lend long; forward rates then exceed expected future short-term rates. The third theory is the most consistent with the evidence that zero curves slope upward more often than downward. Its practical implication for this book is a caution: a forward rate is a price at which a future rate can be fixed today, and it should not be read as an unbiased forecast.\nConcept check 3.3"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.8#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.8", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.8", "heading": "Theories of the term structure", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.8 Theories of the term structure", "body": "A bond has a price of $940 and a duration of 6.5 years, with the yield measured with continuous compounding. If the yield falls by 0.20 percentage points, the price is expected to:"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:3.8#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:3.8", "chapter": 3, "section": "3.8", "heading": "Theories of the term structure", "kind": "notes", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — 3.8 Theories of the term structure", "body": "Historical note\nDuration was introduced by Frederick Macaulay in 1938 in a study of American bond yields for the National Bureau of Economic Research, as a better measure of the life of a bond than its maturity. Its use as a measure of price sensitivity became general in the 1970s, when interest rates became far more variable.\nFor some three decades LIBOR was the reference rate for the majority of the world's interest rate derivatives. Investigations beginning in 2012 established that some submitting banks had misstated their borrowing costs, and regulators concluded that a rate based on judgment in a market with few transactions could not be made reliable. The transition to transaction-based overnight rates was among the largest coordinated changes ever made to financial contracts."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Summary#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Summary", "chapter": 3, "section": "Summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — Summary Summary", "body": "Derivatives are valued with a risk-free rate obtained from overnight reference rates such as SOFR, SONIA, and €STR through the overnight indexed swap market.\nA quoted rate is defined only together with its compounding frequency. Rates convert by \\(R_c=m\\ln(1+R_m/m)\\) and \\(R_m=m(e^{R_c/m}-1)\\); with continuous compounding the discount factor is \\(e^{-RT}\\).\nThe zero rate for a maturity applies to a single payment at that maturity. A coupon bond is priced by discounting each payment at its own zero rate; its yield is the single rate that reproduces the price, and the par yield is the coupon that prices it at par.\nThe bootstrap method extracts zero rates from bond prices in order of maturity.\nThe forward rate between \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\) is \\((R_2T_2-R_1T_1)/(T_2-T_1)\\). It lies above the zero rates when the zero curve rises and below them when it falls, and it can be secured today.\nDuration is the value-weighted average time of a bond's cash flows, and \\(\\Delta B/B\\approx-D\\,\\Delta y\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 3, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — Exercises Exercises", "body": "3.1 A bank quotes 7 percent per year with monthly compounding. Find (a) the effective annual rate and (b) the equivalent continuously compounded rate.\n(a) \\((1+0.07/12)^{12}-1=7.229\\%\\). (b) \\(12\\ln(1+0.07/12)=6.980\\%\\).\n3.2 A deposit pays 4.5 percent with continuous compounding, with interest paid out semiannually. What interest is paid each half-year on $250,000?\n\\(R_2=2(e^{0.045/2}-1)=4.551\\%\\). Interest per half-year: \\(250{,}000\\times0.04551/2=\\$5{,}689\\).\n3.3 Using the zero rates of Table 3.1, price a 1.5-year bond with principal $100 and a coupon of 6 percent per year paid semiannually.\n\\(3\\times0.980199+3\\times0.956954+103\\times0.931928=2.9406+2.8709+95.9886=\\$101.80\\).\n3.4 Using Table 3.1, find the 1.5-year par yield with semiannual compounding.\n\\(A=0.980199+0.956954+0.931928=2.869081\\). \\(c=(100-93.1928)\\times2/2.869081=4.745\\). The par yield is 4.745 percent.\n3.5 A six-month zero-coupon bond is priced at $98.00 and a one-year zero-coupon bond at $95.50 (principal $100). A 1.5-year bond paying a coupon of 4 percent per year semiannually is priced at $99.20. Find the 1.5-year zero rate."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 3, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — Exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(2\\times0.9800+2\\times0.9550=3.870\\). \\(102\\,d_{1.5}=99.20-3.870=95.330\\), so \\(d_{1.5}=0.934608\\) and \\(R=-\\ln(0.934608)/1.5=4.509\\%\\).\n3.6 Zero rates with continuous compounding are 4.0 percent for two years and 4.6 percent for three years. (a) Find the forward rate for the third year. (b) Show how $1,000,000 can be invested during the third year at this rate by transactions made today.\n(a) \\((4.6\\times3-4.0\\times2)/1=5.8\\%\\). (b) Borrow \\(1{,}000{,}000e^{-0.08}=\\$923{,}116\\) for two years at 4.0 percent and invest it for three years at 4.6 percent. Net cash flow today is zero. At the end of year 2 the loan requires $1,000,000; at the end of year 3 the investment pays \\(923{,}116e^{0.138}=\\$1{,}059{,}715\\). The return in the third year is \\(\\ln(1.059715)=5.8\\%\\).\n3.7 A three-year bond pays an annual coupon of $8 on a principal of $100 and has a yield of 6 percent with continuous compounding. Find its price and duration, and estimate the price if the yield rises to 6.25 percent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 3, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 3, Interest Rates, Discounting, and Forward Rates — Exercises Exercises", "body": "Present values: \\(8e^{-0.06}=7.5341\\); \\(8e^{-0.12}=7.0954\\); \\(108e^{-0.18}=90.2092\\). Price: $104.84. Time-weighted sum: \\(7.5341+2\\times7.0954+3\\times90.2092=292.352\\). Duration: \\(292.352/104.84=2.789\\) years. \\(\\Delta B\\approx-104.84\\times2.789\\times0.0025=-0.731\\); estimated price $104.11.\n3.8 ★ The zero curve is \\(R(T)=0.03+0.004T\\) for \\(0\\le T\\le5\\). Find the instantaneous forward rate at \\(T=3\\) and the forward rate for the period from year 3 to year 4, and comment.\n\\(R_F(T)=R+T\\,dR/dT=0.03+0.008T\\), so \\(R_F(3)=5.4\\%\\). For years 3 to 4: \\(R(3)=4.2\\%\\), \\(R(4)=4.6\\%\\); \\((4.6\\times4-4.2\\times3)/1=5.8\\%\\), which equals the average of the instantaneous forward rate over the period, \\(0.03+0.008\\times3.5=5.8\\%\\). Both exceed the zero rates because the curve slopes upward."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
